.; <year>2006</year></citation></ref><ref id="R17"><label>17. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Paton</surname> <given-names>AW</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Paton</surname> <given-names>JC</given-names></string-name> <article-title>Detection and characterization of Shiga toxigenic <italic>Escherichia coli</italic> by using multiplex PCR assays for <italic>stx1, stx2, eaeA,</italic> enterohemorrhagic <italic>E. coli hlyA, rfbO111,</italic> and <italic>rfbO157.</italic></article-title><source>J Clin Microbiol</source> <year>1998</year>;<volume>36</volume>:<fpage>598</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>602</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9466788</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R18"><label>18. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Keskim&#x000e4;ki</surname> <given-names>M</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Eklund</surname> <given-names>M</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Pesonen</surname> <given-names>H</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Heiskanen</surname> <given-names>T</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Siitonen</surname> <given-names>A</given-names></string-name> <article-title>Study Group. EPEC, EAEC and STEC in stool specimens: prevalence and molecular epidemiology of isolates.</article-title><source>Diagn Microbiol Infect Dis</source> <year>2001</year>;<volume>40</volume>:<fpage>151</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>6</lpage> <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0732-8893(01)00265-6</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11576786</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R19"><label>19. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Frenzen</surname> <given-names>PD</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Riggs</surname> <given-names>TL</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Buzby</surname> <given-names>JC</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Breuer</surname> <given-names>T</given-names></string-name>,